// #88, review round 5: a generic call's arguments meet its INSTANTIATED
// parameters in a generic body too.
//
// `take({ .. 1 }, r)` is refused for an unrelated reason (the block's
// `let k: i64 = true`), so the walk of the instantiated body asks each of its
// parts again -- and it only inferred from an argument given for a parameter
// naming `T`, never asking it against the parameter the instantiation made of
// it. At `T = i64`, `b: &T` is `b: &i64`, a parameter WRITTEN `&T`, for which
// code generation takes the address of what it is given: `held`, a held
// reference, became a pointer to the pointer. Measured on main (2ca5eb5) and
// on round 5 alike: internal compiler error, the C backend emitted ill-typed
// C. (The width spelling, `take(1, &n)` with `n: u32`, is refused by the same
// question, uncoded, as the checker refuses it outside a generic body.)
fn take<T>(a: T, b: &T) -> i64 {
    7
}

fn f<U>(x: U) -> i64 {
    let n = 5;
    let held = &n;
    take({ let k: i64 = true; 1 }, held)
}

fn main() {
    let n = 5;
    print_int(take(1, &n));
    print_int(f(1));
}
